Feature: Presets fitted to the song corpus sit inside the records' ranges
  The song corpus (song-corpus/BRIEF.md) measured the separated stems of excerpts of the user's own
  records with one extractor, song-corpus/scripts/features.py. tests/corpus_gate.hpp measures a
  render as that extractor does (its pyin port in tests/corpus_pyin.hpp), and
  tests/corpus_reference.hpp holds the records' quartiles, and the University of Iowa tuba's taped
  takes, numbers only, generated by tools/corpus_reference.py from features_ref.json and
  build/honkr-corpus-render iowa-tuba. See docs/CORPUS.md.

  Executable: tests/corpus_gate_tests.cpp (CTest corpus_gate_tests).

  # the_corpus_measures_match_the_corpus_extractor_on_known_signals
  Scenario: The corpus measures read known signals as the corpus extractor does
    Given a 440 Hz sine on the left only, and a 220 Hz sine whose pitch swings 20 cents at 6 Hz, in both channels, sounding 0.8 s of every second
    Then they read as features.py reads the same signals: centroid 439.98 Hz and roll-off 441.43 Hz (the bin above), correlation 0 against silence and 1 between equal channels, wobble 11.08 cents

  # every_corpus_preset_sits_inside_the_records_ranges
  Scenario: Every corpus preset sits inside the records' ranges
    Given each preset fitted to the corpus playing its phrase: a horn section's three lines in counterpoint, or the tuba's line, 16 s at 44.1 kHz
    Then its spectral centroid, 85% roll-off, L/R correlation and tape wobble lie inside the 25th to 75th percentile of every group of excerpts it answers to, measured as that stem; the tuba's spectrum inside the range of the real tuba's taped takes
    And the horn sections answer to OTC's `other` stems, both the five whose stems plausibly carry horns and all twelve
    And the tuba's centroid and roll-off answer to the University of Iowa tuba playing the same line through the same bedroom, microphones and tape, between its mezzo-forte and fortissimo takes; its L/R correlation and wobble to OTC's `bass` stems, which hold no tuba (a real one separated by htdemucs_6s raises a bass stem's centroid from about 120 Hz to 280 or more, or lands in `guitar`)

  # the_home_taped_tuba_is_recorded_as_the_real_tuba_it_answers_to
  Scenario: The home-taped tuba is recorded as the real tuba it answers to
    Given the Home-taped tuba preset, and the bedroom, microphone pair and tape the Iowa tuba's taped takes went through
    Then its room, microphone distance, pattern and setup, wow and flutter and tape bandwidth are those, so the two differ only in the instrument

  # the_tuba_played_dry_sounds_where_the_recorded_tuba_does
  Scenario: The tuba played dry sounds where the recorded tuba does
    Given HONKR's tuba playing the tuba line dry (no room, the microphone on the bell, no vibrato, the master 18 dB down so the output limiter never acts) at velocity 0.55 and 0.95, and the University of Iowa tuba's mezzo-forte and fortissimo notes spliced into the same line (anechoic, 1.5 m)
    Then measured as the corpus measures, each dynamic's spectral centroid and 85% roll-off lie within 0.3 octave of the recording's

  # what_is_not_a_taped_tuba_fails_the_tubas_ranges
  Scenario: What is not a taped tuba fails the tuba's ranges
    Given the tuba's line played by a studio tuba (the Tuba preset: no tape, a hall), by the Home-taped tuba with its tape taken off, and by a bass clarinet recorded as the Home-taped tuba is
    Then each falls outside a range the Home-taped tuba answers to
